{"step_index":152,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T02:02:48Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T10:02:48+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T10:02:49+08:00\nShowing lines 1-57 of 57\n1: # EV high-spec architecture diagrams\n2: \n3: `index.html` is a self-contained page with the A-platform electrical/electronic and IVI hardware views, the B-platform system view, the high-level software view, and its OTA, Diagnostic, Audio, Widevine DRM, and Display child architectures. Open it directly in a browser. Click **OTA & Update Management** or **OTA Service** in the high-level software view to open the OTA architecture; `?tab=software&view=ota` opens OTA directly; click **Diagnostics & Observability**, **Manufacturing Diagnostics**, or **Dcm** for Diagnostics, or use `?tab=software&view=diag`. The OTA diagram now shows directed component interactions and status return; its path controls highlight QNX, Android, MCU, or feedback routes. Empty layers are omitted. Module rows use an aligned grid with orthogonal routes; matched `SPI` and `PKG` ports represent the same connection at its two endpoints, and gaps at crossings indicate no junction. MCU internals are marked as reference-only because the firmware source is not present in this workspace.\n4: \n5: Edit the Python (including `software_ota.py`, `software_ota_graph.py`, `software_diagnostics.py`, `software_diagnostics_graph.py`, `software_audio.py`, `software_audio_graph.py`, `software_widevine.py`, and `software_widevine_graph.py`, `software_display.py`, and `software_display_graph.py`), HTML, CSS, JavaScript, or TSV sources in this directory, then regenerate the page:\n6: \n7: ```sh\n8: python3 build.py\n9: ```\n10: \n11: The build also refreshes `architecture.svg`, `ivi-hardware.svg`, and `b-platform-system.svg`. Commit the edited sources and regenerated outputs together. `template.html` is a build template; open `index.html` for the finished page.\n12: \n13: The B-platform diagram is a vector redraw of the supplied system overview and enlarged detail images. It preserves the three configuration columns, component part numbers, connector positions, branch top\n<truncated 9114 bytes>\ndeselection with OTA. `software_display.py` records responsibilities and source evidence; `software_display_graph.py` renders QNX/AAOS layers, OpenWFD/HAB interactions, HGSL/GSL GPU virtualization, buffer references, event/fence return, and the hardware pixel path. QDI/MDP/MDSS, DSI/DP drivers and HAL, and board libraries are components inside the OpenWFD server. The hardware band separately shows the DSI/DP Controller & PHY; control routes configure the hardware while the pixel path runs from the DPU through the controller and physical link to the panel. Screen Configuration (`graphics.conf`) and OpenWFD Configuration (`qcdisplaycfg.xml`) are separate static inputs, connected only to QNX Screen and OpenWFD Server respectively. Guest acquire and output fences remain local. Board configuration defines panel assignment and physical output; Linux-host Weston, VirtIO-GPU and vhost components are outside this HQX/QNX baseline.\n54: \n55: The Display GPU branch follows the GSL Host/Guest reference naming: Android OpenGL/EGL Subdriver and Vulkan Driver feed GSL Client, HGSL and KHAB; QNX separates UHAB/HAB transport from GSL HAB Server → GSL (`libGSLUser.so`) → KGSL. The current Android 13 source supports HWUI SkiaGL/SkiaVulkan, while RenderEngine uses GLES/SkiaGL. Shared command queues and TCSR/GMU doorbell are HGSL capabilities; HAB ports represent control RPC rather than every GPU submission. `content/gunyah/graphics.md` informs the rendering layers; Gunyah `gh_dbl` and Android 16/Graphite details are not part of this HQX/QNX source baseline.\n56: \n57: The Display Android domain follows Java, Native, HAL and Linux Kernel bands: UI traversal/recording at the top, the GPU rendering stack on the left, and Surface → BLAST → SurfaceFlinger → Composer on the right. Native render clients and API loaders are explicit. Gralloc, HWC, SDM and the DRM adapter occupy the HAL band; HGSL and the WFD frontend share Guest HAB through distinct MM_GFX/MM_DISP channels. Matched GFX/DISP ports keep those host connections readable.","truncated_fields":["content"]}
